 the main text).\nBy changing the model \u03c4 on a given dataset, we are effectively changing the difficulty of the task that the networks have to solve. Transients in the network will be relevant on timescales that scale as \u223c \u03c4 ; therefore, for very large \u03c4 , the velocity is suppressed and h evolves very slowly. This places a greater burden on F\u03b8 to send small changes in the phase space into effectively orthogonal vectors in the OU time series. Therefore, we suspected that in the transient regime, the complexity of F\u03b8 becomes more important for fitting noise. Confirming our intuition, we see that as \u03c4 increases, the performance gap between networks that have more complex F\u03b8 (i.e., nODEs and gnODEs) and networks with simpler F\u03b8 (i.e., RNNs, mGRUs and GRUs) becomes larger (Figure 3D\u2013F)."
